steps.body:
OWASP gives broken authorization three of its top five places, because it's the easiest bug to write and the easiest to find. Finding it takes no special tools, only a changed number in a URL or an extra field in a body. It also slips past tests that only ever sign in as the owner, which is most tests.

## Objects: whose booking is it?

**Object level authorization** asks, for each object a request touches, whether this caller may touch this one. Its failure is called **BOLA**, Broken Object Level Authorization, and in code it usually looks like this:

```java
// bookings/BookingController.java (the bug)
@GetMapping("/{bookingId}")
Map<String, Object> get(@PathVariable long bookingId) {
    return jdbc.sql("SELECT * FROM bookings WHERE id = ?")
        .param(bookingId)
        .query()
        .singleRow();
}
```

The SQL even uses a parameter, so it's safe from injection, and it's still wide open. Nothing is wrong with the login either: Ada is who she says she is. The handler just never asks whose booking it loaded, so any signed-in member can read any booking. Roombook's ids are numbers, so trying 1003, 1004, and on is a five-line loop.

The fix is the ownership check from the lesson on permissions, with the caller in the query itself:

```java
// bookings/JdbcBookingRepository.java
@Override
public Optional<Booking> findForUser(long id, RoombookPrincipal caller) {
    return jdbc.sql("SELECT " + COLUMNS + " FROM bookings WHERE id = :id AND user_id = :userId")
        .param("id", id)
        .param("userId", caller.userId())
        .query(Booking.class)
        .optional();
}
```

A stranger's booking is never even loaded, so nothing further down can leak it by mistake. The answer is 404, the same as for a booking that doesn't exist, since a 403 would confirm that 1003 is real. The full rule in the lesson on permissions adds two more branches to the same `WHERE`, for admins and for staff in the booking's building. The first example below shows Ada's two answers.

### Make the safe way the only way

A check that every handler has to remember is a check someone will forget. Three habits make it hard to forget:

- Give owned data a repository method that takes the principal, like `findForUser(id, caller)`, so a handler can't load a booking without saying who's asking.
- Make new routes deny by default: they need a signed-in caller unless they're public on purpose. The authentication lesson ended its rules with `anyRequest().authenticated()`. This lesson tightens them: the public paths are named one by one, every other `/v1` path needs a signed-in caller, and the last rule is `anyRequest().denyAll()`, so a path nobody wrote a rule for is closed, as the snippet in the functions section below shows.
- Keep PostgreSQL's row-level security from the lesson on permissions underneath, as a safety net.

Random ids like UUIDs make guessing harder, but they aren't a fix. Ids leak in links, logs, and screenshots, and an id nobody can guess, with no check behind it, is still a missing check.

## Fields: which parts may they touch?

**Property level authorization** asks the same question about each field. OWASP calls its failure **BOPLA**, Broken Object Property Level Authorization, and it breaks in two directions:

- **Mass assignment** is copying a request body onto an object wholesale, so fields the client was never meant to set come along: `userId`, `status`, `version`, or a user's `role`. In Spring, it's binding `@RequestBody` straight to a class with a setter or a field for every column, so Jackson fills in `userId` because the class happens to have one.
- **Excessive data exposure** is the reverse: a response that sends every column, `password_hash` included, and trusts the client to show only some. The `SELECT *` in the first snippet does exactly that.

The defenses are ones you've met, now with a security reason. An input schema per operation lists only the fields the caller may set and refuses the rest (the validation lesson), and an output view lists only the fields the caller may see (the lesson on request data):

```java
// bookings/BookingPatch.java
public record BookingPatch(
        @Min(1) @Max(30) Integer attendees,
        @Size(max = 500) String note) {}

// bookings/BookingController.java
private static final Set<String> PATCHABLE = Arrays.stream(BookingPatch.class.getRecordComponents())
        .map(RecordComponent::getName)
        .collect(Collectors.toUnmodifiableSet());

@PatchMapping("/{bookingId}")
public BookingView update(@PathVariable long bookingId, @RequestBody JsonNode patch,
                          @AuthenticationPrincipal Principal principal) {
    List<FieldViolation> unknown = patch.propertyNames().stream()
            .filter(name -> !PATCHABLE.contains(name))
            .map(name -> new FieldViolation(name, "is not a known field"))
            .toList();
    if (!unknown.isEmpty()) {
        throw UnprocessableException.invalidFields(unknown);
    }
    Booking booking = bookings.findForUser(bookingId, principal)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new NotFoundException("No booking " + bookingId + "."));
    int attendees = patch.has("attendees") ? patch.get("attendees").asInt() : booking.attendees();
    String note = patch.has("note") ? patch.get("note").stringValue(null) : booking.note();
    return BookingView.from(bookings.update(booking.withChanges(attendees, note)));
}

// bookings/BookingView.java
public record BookingView(long id, long roomId, long userId, Instant startsAt, Instant endsAt,
                          int attendees, String note, BookingStatus status, int version, Instant createdAt) {
    public static BookingView from(Booking b) {
        return new BookingView(b.id(), b.roomId(), b.userId(), b.startsAt(), b.endsAt(),
            b.attendees(), b.note(), b.status(), b.version(), b.createdAt());
    }
}
```

The PATCH reads its body as a `JsonNode`, as in the lesson on request data, since that's how it tells a missing note from `"note": null`. A `JsonNode` accepts any JSON at all, though, so the `fail-on-unknown-properties` setting from the validation lesson doesn't reach it: that setting only applies when Jackson binds a body to a class, like the create request's record. So the handler checks the names itself, before anything else, against `PATCHABLE`, the fields `BookingPatch` declares, read from the record so the two can't drift apart. Every other field becomes a `FieldViolation`, and `UnprocessableException.invalidFields` reports them all at once as the `validation-failed` problem, as the second example shows. Only then does it load the booking, with the owner in the query, and apply the two fields it knows. On the way out, `BookingView.from` is the only shape a booking leaves in, so a column added to the table later stays inside until someone adds it here on purpose.

## Functions: may they call it at all?

**Function level authorization** is about whole operations: creating rooms is for staff, and deleting them is for admins. Its failure, **BFLA**, happens when a route has no role check: often an admin route added in a hurry, or a DELETE that sits next to a protected GET with nothing of its own. The fix is a role check declared with every route (the lesson on permissions):

```java
// config/SecurityConfig.java, the route rules both chains share
.authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
    .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.GET, "/healthz", "/v1/buildings", "/v1/rooms", "/v1/rooms/*").permitAll()
    .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.POST, "/v1/rooms").hasAnyRole("STAFF", "ADMIN")
    .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.PUT, "/v1/rooms/*").hasAnyRole("STAFF", "ADMIN")
    .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.PATCH, "/v1/rooms/*").hasAnyRole("STAFF", "ADMIN")
    .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.DELETE, "/v1/rooms/*").hasRole("ADMIN")
    .requestMatchers("/v1/**").authenticated()
    .anyRequest().denyAll())
```

Rules match from the top, and the first match wins, so the specific ones come first and `denyAll()` comes last. Keeping every rule in one place means a review sees the whole map at once. A member who calls one of these gets 403. Unlike a booking, a route isn't a secret, since it's in the docs, so there's nothing for a 404 to hide.

## Tests that prove it

These bugs hide from tests that sign in as the owner, so write the attacker's tests too: sign in as Ada, ask for something that isn't hers, and expect a refusal. One for each endpoint that takes an id, and one for each role-protected route, turns this part into something CI checks on every change. `ADA` is the same stand-in for Ada's access token that the testing lesson used:

```java
// src/test/java/dev/hackuniversity/roombook/AuthorizationTests.java
@SpringBootTest
@AutoConfigureMockMvc
class AuthorizationTests {
    static final RequestPostProcessor ADA = jwt().jwt(j -> j.subject("1").claim("role", "member"));

    @Autowired MockMvcTester mvc;

    @Test
    void hidesAnotherMembersBooking() {
        assertThat(mvc.get().uri("/v1/bookings/1003").with(ADA))
            .hasStatus(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND);
    }

    @Test
    void refusesRoomCreationToMembers() {
        assertThat(mvc.post().uri("/v1/rooms")
                .with(ADA)
                .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
                .content("""
                    {"name": "Noether", "building": "main-library", "capacity": 4}
                    """))
            .hasStatus(HttpStatus.FORBIDDEN);
    }
}
```

