steps.body:
Some attacks break nothing. They use the API exactly as designed, only far more than you planned for, or they point Roombook's own requests at places you never meant it to go. Four of the ten risks are like this, and their defenses are limits and allowlists more than checks.

## Resource consumption: put a ceiling on everything

Every request costs something: CPU, memory, database time, and sometimes money, since emails and texts are paid per message. OWASP's API4 is any request that costs much more than it should. The defense is a ceiling on every input that drives a cost, enforced before the expensive part:

| What | Roombook's ceiling | Where |
|---|---|---|
| Body size | 100 KB, 413 above | the proxy and `BodyLimitInterceptor` |
| Page size | `limit` 1 to 100, default 20 | the query schema (the validation lesson) |
| String and list lengths | a note is at most 500 characters, a list of amenities at most 10 | the body schema |
| Time per request | a 2 second budget, with database and outgoing timeouts (the reliability lesson) | the server and each client |
| Requests per caller | per IP, user, and API key, 429 above (the Redis lesson) | the rate limiter |
| Costly operations | logins, signups, and password resets limited per account and per IP | their routes |

In Spring, the Bean Validation annotations from the validation lesson hold the lengths and the page size, and `spring.servlet.multipart.max-file-size` caps uploads. Tomcat's `server.tomcat.max-http-form-post-size` only covers form posts, not JSON, which is why the request data lesson added `BodyLimitInterceptor`, refusing a `Content-Length` over 100 KB with a 413. That left one gap: a body sent in chunks, with no `Content-Length` at all. The reverse proxy in front of Roombook closes it by counting the bytes that actually arrive (nginx's `client_max_body_size`, for example), so the two together cover every body.

## Business flows: features used by machines

OWASP's API6 isn't a bug in any one line. Booking a room is a real feature, and a script that books every free room for two weeks uses it perfectly, and ruins it for everyone else. The fix is a rule that matches how the feature should be used, enforced on the server. Roombook already has one: a member holds at most three upcoming bookings, which the lesson on data integrity counts inside the booking transaction with `countUpcoming`, so two quick requests can't both slip under the limit. A fourth gets 409 with the `booking-limit` problem type.

Other flows get rules of their own. Sign-ups get a rate limit per IP and an email to confirm, and cancelling and rebooking the same room over and over gets noticed by an alert (the next lesson). Ask of each feature: what would a machine doing this a thousand times look like, and how much would that hurt?

## SSRF: requests Roombook makes for someone else

**SSRF**, Server Side Request Forgery, is API7. It happens when your server fetches a URL that a caller chose. Roombook does exactly that with webhooks (the reliability lesson): an admin registers a URL, and Roombook POSTs events to it.

Roombook's servers sit inside a private network, so they can reach places no outsider can: the database, internal admin tools, and in most clouds a **metadata service** at `169.254.169.254`, which hands out the server's own credentials to whoever asks from inside. A webhook URL that points there turns Roombook into the attacker's hands inside your network.

```diagram security-ssrf
The sender checks the address it's about to connect to, so private addresses are refused even when a public name points at them.
```

The defense has a few layers, and the order matters:

1. When a URL is registered, accept only `https`, on port 443, with a real hostname.
2. When Roombook connects, check the **IP address it's about to connect to**, not the URL's text, and refuse private, loopback, and link-local ranges. Checking the text isn't enough: `http://[::ffff:127.0.0.1]` is loopback in disguise, and a hostname can resolve to a public address when you check it and a private one a second later, a trick called **DNS rebinding**. Only the address at connect time is the truth.
3. Don't follow redirects, since a redirect is a second URL nobody checked.
4. Use short timeouts, and never show the response body to the caller, so Roombook can't be used to read internal pages either.

In Spring, Roombook sends webhooks with a `RestClient` built on Apache HttpClient 5, because it lets you replace the step that turns a hostname into addresses. The replacement refuses to hand back anything that isn't public, and the client connects only to what it hands back:

```java
// webhooks/PublicOnlyDnsResolver.java
import java.net.Inet6Address;
import java.net.InetAddress;
import java.net.UnknownHostException;

import org.apache.hc.client5.http.SystemDefaultDnsResolver;

class PublicOnlyDnsResolver extends SystemDefaultDnsResolver {
    @Override
    public InetAddress[] resolve(String host) throws UnknownHostException {
        InetAddress[] addresses = super.resolve(host);
        for (InetAddress address : addresses) {
            if (!isPublic(address)) {
                throw new UnknownHostException("refusing to connect to " + address.getHostAddress());
            }
        }
        return addresses;
    }

    static boolean isPublic(InetAddress a) {
        if (a.isAnyLocalAddress() || a.isLoopbackAddress() || a.isLinkLocalAddress()
                || a.isSiteLocalAddress() || a.isMulticastAddress()) {
            return false;
        }
        byte[] b = a.getAddress();
        if (a instanceof Inet6Address) {
            return (b[0] & 0xfe) != 0xfc;
        }
        return !((b[0] & 0xff) == 100 && (b[1] & 0xc0) == 64);
    }
}
```

The JDK's own checks cover loopback, link-local, and the private IPv4 ranges, and the two byte tests add the IPv6 private range `fc00::/7` and the shared `100.64.0.0/10`. HttpClient asks the resolver for IP addresses typed into a URL too, so `http://169.254.169.254` is refused like any name. The client itself is a bean with redirects off and short timeouts:

```java
// webhooks/WebhookClientConfig.java
@Bean
RestClient webhookClient() {
    var connections = PoolingHttpClientConnectionManagerBuilder.create()
        .setDnsResolver(new PublicOnlyDnsResolver())
        .setDefaultConnectionConfig(ConnectionConfig.custom()
            .setConnectTimeout(Timeout.ofSeconds(5))
            .setSocketTimeout(Timeout.ofSeconds(10))
            .build())
        .build();
    var httpClient = HttpClients.custom()
        .setConnectionManager(connections)
        .setDefaultRequestConfig(RequestConfig.custom().setRedirectsEnabled(false).build())
        .build();
    return RestClient.builder()
        .requestFactory(new HttpComponentsClientHttpRequestFactory(httpClient))
        .build();
}
```

It needs the `org.apache.httpcomponents.client5:httpclient5` dependency, whose version Spring Boot manages. Posting to `http://localhost:28481/admin` through this client fails with `refusing to connect to 127.0.0.1`, and `http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/` with `refusing to connect to 169.254.169.254`, both before any connection opens. In Roombook, a refused delivery is recorded in `webhook_deliveries` as failed, with the reason, and never retried.

## Other APIs' answers are input too

API10, unsafe consumption, is SSRF's mirror image. When Roombook calls another API, like a campus calendar service, the answer is input from outside your trust boundary, however reputable the company. Check its shape with a schema, as you do for requests, cap its size, set timeouts, keep TLS certificate checks on, and never pass its text into SQL, a shell, or a page unescaped. A partner that gets hacked shouldn't get you hacked too.

